<header><p class="st-kicker">prince bishopric · Germany and Central Europe</p><h1 class="st-title">Prince-Bishopric of Hildesheim</h1><p class="st-meta">1235 CE – 1803 CE (approx.) · capital Hildesheim</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">Lower Saxon church state famed for Bishop Bernward's Romanesque art, which lost most of its land in a 1519 feud and won it back in 1643.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/louis-the-pious">Louis the Pious</a> founded the diocese of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hildesheim">Hildesheim</a> in 815. Around 1000 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bishop">Bishop</a> <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bernward-of-hildesheim">Bernward</a> made the city a centre of art, building <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/st-michaels-church-hildesheim">St. Michael's Church</a> and commissioning its bronze doors and column. The bishops built up a territory between the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/leine">Leine</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/oker">Oker</a>, and by the 13th century, around 1235, they ruled as imperial princes.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>The cathedral chapter and estates shared power with the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bishop">bishops</a>. In the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hildesheim-diocesan-feud">Hildesheim Diocesan Feud</a> of 1519 to 1523 the see lost most of its territory to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/house-of-welf">Guelph</a> dukes, retaining only the Small Diocese. The city of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hildesheim">Hildesheim</a> turned Lutheran, while the bishops stayed Catholic; <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/house-of-wittelsbach">Wittelsbach</a> princes such as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/clemens-august-of-bavaria">Clemens August of Bavaria</a> often held the see alongside <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/free-imperial-city-of-cologne">Cologne</a>.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>The lost lands were restored in 1643. The see was secularised in 1803 and given to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-prussia">Prussia</a>, then belonged to the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/kingdom-of-westphalia">Kingdom of Westphalia</a>, and in 1815 to the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/kingdom-of-hanover">Kingdom of Hanover</a>. Its cathedral and St. Michael's are a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/unesco-world-heritage-site">UNESCO World Heritage Site</a>.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/prince-bishopric-of-hildesheim.jpg" alt="Prince-Bishopric of Hildesheim" width="320" height="300"><figcaption>Image: NordNordWest · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Herzogtum_Braunschweig_1789.png" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>1235 CE – 1803 CE (approx.)</dd><dt>Centuries</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/13th-century">13th century</a> – <a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>prince bishopric</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>theocratic</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Hildesheim</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Bernward of Hildesheim, Clemens August of Bavaria</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Catholicism, Lutheranism</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Low German</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/germany">Germany</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Hochstift Hildesheim</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=1519&amp;focus=prince-bishopric-of-hildesheim" aria-label="Prince-Bishopric of Hildesheim on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="4904 376 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Prince-Bishopric of Hildesheim lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-tie" d="M5224 632L5229 694M5224 632L5300 622"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M5229 694h0M5300 622h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5224 632h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5224 632h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks Hildesheim. <a href="/atlas?year=1519&amp;focus=prince-bishopric-of-hildesheim">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>
